import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Two Rust photo apps in the browser — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="LightCraft is another wonderful Lightroom replacement written in Rust that runs in a browser tab. Here's what I learned by putting it next to LightPhotos, with some measurements, though they aren't quite an apples-to-apples comparison."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-10-07">October 7, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Two Rust photo apps in the browser</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          <a href="https://github.com/storytold/lightcraft">LightCraft</a>
          is another wonderful Lightroom replacement, written in Rust, that
          runs in a browser tab. Here's what I learned by putting the two
          approaches side by side, with some measurements, though to be fair
          they aren't quite an apples-to-apples comparison.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 8 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          Both apps are Rust and egui compiled to WebAssembly, and they
          download about the same amount: 3.5&nbsp;MB and 3.8&nbsp;MB
          compressed. On twenty Sony RAW files, LightPhotos filled its grid
          in 1.4&nbsp;s and showed each photo in about half a second.
          LightCraft took 2.3&nbsp;s and about 1.9&nbsp;s, using nearly
          twice the memory. That isn't the same work done faster, though.
          At fit-to-window, LightPhotos applies your edits to the JPEG the
          camera already made, and only decodes the RAW data when you zoom
          in. LightCraft develops the RAW data itself every time.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>The language doesn't decide much.</strong> Same language,
          same interface library, same compile target, and the two apps
          still split on threads, graphics, storage and toolchain.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Most of the speed gap is a choice about what to
          start from.</strong> Editing the camera's embedded JPEG is quicker
          than developing the RAW data, and at fit-to-window it's enough to
          judge a photo. The RAW data only pays off once you zoom in.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The clock bug is everywhere.</strong> While I was
          measuring, LightCraft crashed on the same <code>Instant::now()</code>
          bug I wrote about in my last post. Nothing catches it until the
          code runs in a browser.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What they have in common</h2>
      <p>
        A lot, which is what made the comparison interesting.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Rust, top to bottom.</strong> Decoding, editing, the
          catalog and the interface are all Rust, with no JavaScript
          framework.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>egui for the interface.</strong> Both draw every button and
          slider themselves on a canvas, the same way on the desktop and in
          the browser.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>WebAssembly for the browser.</strong> The web version of
          each is its desktop app compiled to WebAssembly, not a separate web
          app. A fix in one usually fixes the other.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>About the same download.</strong> 3.5&nbsp;MB and
          3.8&nbsp;MB compressed, even though each carries its own RAW
          decoding and editing code.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The main differences</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Your folders, directly.</strong> LightPhotos opens a folder
          you pick and works on the files where they are, so there's no
          import step. LightCraft imports photos by copying them into the
          browser's own storage, which keeps its library there after a
          reload.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Decode on zoom.</strong> At fit-to-window, LightPhotos
          applies your edits to the full-size JPEG most cameras store inside
          a RAW file, and only decodes the RAW data when you zoom in.
          LightCraft develops the RAW data for every view.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>RAW decoder and license.</strong> LightPhotos's own code is
          MIT or Apache-2.0, but it decodes RAW files with rawler, a library
          under the LGPL-2.1. So the web build has to say it uses rawler and
          include that license. (The Mac app decodes with Apple's own
          ImageIO, so it doesn't include rawler.) LightCraft wrote its own
          decoder, so its own code is MIT or Apache-2.0 with no copyleft
          libraries.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>One tab or several.</strong> LightCraft allows one tab at a
          time, so two copies can't overwrite each other's saves. LightPhotos
          doesn't guard against that yet: two tabs on the same folder can
          each save over the other's edits to a photo.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>WebGPU and WebGL2.</strong> LightPhotos draws with WebGPU,
          the browser's newer graphics API. LightCraft uses WebGL2, which
          more browsers support.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Threads and workers.</strong> LightPhotos runs its decodes
          on threads that share one block of memory. LightCraft sends jobs to
          up to four Web Workers, each with its own memory.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Features.</strong> LightCraft has way more of them.
          LightPhotos is for going through a folder fast, keeping the good
          ones and giving them quick edits. LightCraft is a full
          Lightroom-style editor: masks, tone curves, colour grading,
          healing, lens corrections, presets, versions and history, HDR and
          panorama merges, and export to six formats. It also organizes your
          photos into albums, smart albums and stacks.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 236" role="img" aria-label="Two ways to lay out the browser build. LightPhotos: the main thread reads your folder through the File System Access API and hands file bytes to decode threads that share one block of memory, drawing with WebGPU. It needs cross-origin isolation headers and a nightly Rust toolchain. LightCraft: the main thread copies imported photos into the browser's own storage and sends jobs to up to four Web Workers, each with its own memory, drawing with WebGL2. It needs no special headers and builds on stable Rust.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">LIGHTPHOTOS</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="0" y="26" width="300" height="56" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="48">Main thread, WebGPU</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="68">reads your folder in place</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="92" width="300" height="70" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="114">6 decode threads</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="134">one shared memory (SharedArrayBuffer)</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="152">same queues as the desktop app</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="186">Needs: COOP/COEP headers, nightly Rust</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="204">Browsers: Chrome, Edge</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="340" y="12">LIGHTCRAFT</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="340" y="26" width="300" height="56" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="356" y="48">Main thread, WebGL2</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="356" y="68">copies photos into browser storage</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="340" y="92" width="300" height="70" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="356" y="114">4 Web Workers</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="356" y="134">each its own copy, its own memory</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="356" y="152">jobs in as JSON, pixels back</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="340" y="186">Needs: nothing special, stable Rust</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="340" y="204">Browsers: no WebGPU or folder access needed</text>

          <line class="lp-fig-line-soft" x1="320" y1="0" x2="320" y2="216"></line>
        </svg>
      </figure>

      <p>
        LightCraft's layout looks familiar. It's what LightPhotos did until
        late September, when I described it in <a
        href="/blogs/challenges-with-webassembly.html">challenges with
        WebAssembly</a>: separate workers, no shared memory, no special
        headers.
      </p>

      <p>
        Here are the differences in more detail:
      </p>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick">LightPhotos</th>
              <th scope="col">LightCraft</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Your photos</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Read in place from a folder you pick</td>
              <td>Copied into the browser's own storage on import</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Parallel work</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Threads sharing one memory</td>
              <td>Up to 4 workers, each with its own memory</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Graphics</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">WebGPU</td>
              <td>WebGL2</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Rust toolchain</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Nightly, standard library rebuilt</td>
              <td>Stable</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Server headers</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Required (cross-origin isolation)</td>
              <td>Optional</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">What a RAW shows at fit</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">The camera's embedded JPEG, with your edits</td>
              <td>The RAW data, developed, with your edits</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">RAW decoder</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">rawler (LGPL-2.1), disclosed with its license</td>
              <td>Its own</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">License</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">MIT or Apache-2.0, plus rawler under LGPL-2.1</td>
              <td>MIT or Apache-2.0</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">After a reload</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Reopen Session picks up where you left off</td>
              <td>Library is still there</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Open in several tabs</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Allowed, with no guard against two tabs overwriting each other's saves</td>
              <td>One tab at a time</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>How I measured</h2>
      <p>
        I built both from their current code, served each from its own
        local address, and drove them with Playwright in Chromium 154 on an
        M4 Pro Mac with 48&nbsp;GB of memory. The photos were twenty RAW
        files from a Sony A7 II, 24 megapixels and about 25&nbsp;MB each.
        Neither app had seen them before.
      </p>
      <p>
        A timer starts when I press the key, and stops when the screen stops
        changing. Every 150&nbsp;ms the script takes a screenshot, and once
        three in a row match, it counts as done. That's how
        <code>tools/web-bench</code> already measures LightPhotos, and it
        works the same for any app. It's coarse, though: each number
        includes about 140&nbsp;ms of screenshot overhead, so compare the
        two apps with each other, not with a stopwatch. For the next-photo
        number I pressed the right arrow eight times and took the median.
      </p>

      <h2>The numbers</h2>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick">LightPhotos</th>
              <th scope="col">LightCraft</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Download, compressed (brotli)</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">3.5&nbsp;MB</td>
              <td>3.8&nbsp;MB</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">WebAssembly file, uncompressed</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">14.1&nbsp;MB</td>
              <td>17.1&nbsp;MB</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Fill the grid with 20 RAW thumbnails, total</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">1.4&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>2.3&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Show the next photo full size, each (median of 8)</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.53&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>1.88&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Memory afterwards, all threads and workers</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">1.9&nbsp;GB</td>
              <td>3.4&nbsp;GB</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 172" role="img" aria-label="Time in seconds. Fill grid with 20 RAWs, total: LightPhotos 1.4, LightCraft 2.3. Next photo, each: LightPhotos 0.53, LightCraft 1.88.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">SECONDS, LOWER IS FASTER</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="46">Fill grid with 20 RAWs, total</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="66">LightPhotos</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="120" y="58" width="244" height="10" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="372" y="67">1.4</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="86">LightCraft</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="120" y="78" width="400" height="10" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="528" y="87">2.3</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="122">Next photo full size, each</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="142">LightPhotos</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="120" y="134" width="92" height="10" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="220" y="143">0.53</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="162">LightCraft</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="120" y="154" width="327" height="10" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="455" y="163">1.88</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The two downloads come out close. LightCraft carries a whole editing
        pipeline and its own RAW decoder, and still comes in only
        0.3&nbsp;MB bigger compressed. Its file would be bigger again with
        the Japanese font it embeds in release builds, which I left out.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the numbers differ</h2>
      <p>
        Mostly because of what it starts from. Most cameras store a
        full-size JPEG inside every RAW file, and at fit-to-window that JPEG
        has more pixels than the screen can show. So when you open a photo,
        LightPhotos decodes that JPEG and applies your sliders to it. Only
        when you zoom in does it load the full decode, which demosaics the
        sensor data (turns it into a full colour image) at full resolution.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightCraft never starts from the camera's JPEG. Everything you see
        comes out of its own pipeline, at every zoom level. So it develops
        the RAW data for every view: demosaic, white balance, highlight
        recovery, its colour model, then your edits. That's more work per
        photo, and it means the colours you see at fit come from the same
        place as the colours you get at 100%. Doing all that in under two
        seconds, in a browser, on a 24-megapixel file, is good going.
      </p>
      <p>
        Opening the folder is closer, and the reasons there are the threads
        and the copy. LightPhotos's decode threads pull thumbnails from one
        shared queue, and the photo you're looking at gets priority. When I
        moved to shared memory, the number of thumbnails decoding at once
        went from 1 to 9, because the RAW library I use had been quietly
        running every decode on a single fallback thread. LightCraft, on the
        other hand, copies all 498&nbsp;MB into the browser's storage while
        it imports. That's part of its 2.3&nbsp;s, and it's what lets the
        library survive a reload.
      </p>

      <h2>Why LightCraft uses more memory</h2>
      <p>
        Each LightCraft worker is a separate copy of the program with its
        own memory, and memory a WebAssembly program grows never shrinks
        back. It also keeps recently used originals in memory on the main
        thread, which is the 498&nbsp;MB of RAW files right there. Four
        workers plus that comes to 3.4&nbsp;GB.
      </p>
      <p>
        Shared memory isn't free either. Each LightPhotos decode thread needs
        about 300&nbsp;MB of scratch space for a RAW file, all from one heap
        that's limited to 4&nbsp;GB. With ten threads it peaked at
        3.4&nbsp;GB, the same as LightCraft here. So the browser build is
        capped at six threads, which is where the 1.9&nbsp;GB comes from.
      </p>

      <h2>The same bug, again</h2>
      <p>
        The first time I imported the RAW files into LightCraft, all four
        workers died, then the page did. The console said
        <code>time not implemented on this platform</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        That's the bug from my last post. Rust's standard
        <code>Instant::now()</code> crashes in the browser instead of
        returning an error. LightCraft already uses a small library that
        asks the browser's clock everywhere else, but one timer in its RAW
        loader still called the standard one. Tests run on the desktop,
        where that call works, and the built-in demo photos aren't RAW
        files, so it only showed up once a real RAW file came in. Swapping in
        the browser-safe clock is a one-line fix. I made it in my local copy
        to finish the measurements, so LightCraft's numbers here are with
        that change.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both apps hit the same problem the same way, so I don't think it's
        about being careful. A compiler won't catch it, and a test only
        catches it if it runs in a browser. The better fix is a lint that
        rejects <code>std::time::Instant::now</code> outright, so the
        mistake can't get in at all.
      </p>

      <h2>Wrapping up</h2>
      <p>
        Two apps with the same language and the same interface library
        found quite different answers to the same browser problems.
        LightPhotos leans on the camera's JPEG, your own folders and shared
        memory, so it feels quick when you're going through a shoot.
        LightCraft takes on the whole RAW pipeline and a full Lightroom-style
        feature set, with a library that lives in the browser and a build
        that needs no special server setup. And it got all of that working
        in about a week.
      </p>
      <p>
        I learned something from each, and I'm looking forward to seeing
        where LightCraft goes. Both also run as desktop apps on Mac,
        Windows and Linux, where neither has to live with the browser's
        limits. That comparison is next.
      </p>

